# Independently authored PG CAST target-type sentinel
# slice — TIME-family asymmetry + identifier-type gap +
# NATIONAL CHARACTER parse-reject + 6th hand-rolled
# formatter witness. NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's
# mysql-test/ or PG regress.
#
# Adjacent companion slices (do NOT duplicate):
#   - cast_target_type_aliases_basic_extra.test pins
#     the full MySQL-NAME alias matrix: JSON / STRING
#     working aliases; DATETIME / BLOB / TINYTEXT /
#     BINARY / VARBINARY / YEAR / NVARCHAR / NTEXT
#     SENTINEL rejects; BYTEA plan-vs-runtime split.
#     Also pins SMALLINT / INT2, INTEGER / INT4 / INT8,
#     REAL / FLOAT4, DOUBLE PRECISION / FLOAT8 SUPPORT
#     pins (lines 92-107). This slice does NOT re-pin
#     INT2 / FLOAT4 alias support — already covered
#     there.
#   - cast_basic_extra.test pins INT/INTEGER/BIGINT
#     aliases, TEXT/VARCHAR/CHAR aliases, BYTEA runtime
#     reject, CONVERT(x, SIGNED/UNSIGNED) → 1064.
#   - type_cast_edges_basic_extra.test pins numeric
#     overflow, BIGINT silent-saturation, BOOL/Int,
#     DECIMAL truncation. Includes a `cannot cast to
#     <T>` comment witness for BOOLEAN → INT in header,
#     but no TIMESTAMP/TIME-family target probing.
#   - range_type_basic_extra.test pins `cannot cast to
#     TEXT` comment for range types.
#   - current_schema_citext_basic_extra.test pins
#     `cannot cast to CITEXT` comment for CITEXT alias.
#   - boolean_logic_basic_extra.test references
#     `cannot cast to INT` for BOOLEAN.
#   - PR #427's tstzrange / MULTIRANGE CAST gap slice
#     (adjacent reference) is the IMMEDIATELY adjacent
#     prior CAST-registry gap finding. THIS slice
#     extends to TIME-family and PG identifier types.
#
#   Pre-author dedup grep on tests/main/*.test for
#   TIMETZ / TIME WITH TIME ZONE / TIME WITHOUT TIME
#   ZONE / NCHAR / AS name / AS xid / NATIONAL
#   CHARACTER / cannot cast: NO --error directives on
#   any of these new probe targets. Only comment-level
#   mentions of "cannot cast to <T>" pattern in 4
#   files (range, citext, boolean_logic, type_cast_
#   edges) — none probe the TIMESTAMP-with-float8
#   surface. Per [[feedback_gap_scan_must_grep_all_
#   files]] the case-insensitive grep was the full
#   population.
#
# This slice pins THREE structural findings:
#
#   FINDING 1 — TIME-vs-TIMESTAMP CAST-registry
#   ASYMMETRY. The PG/SQL-standard `TIME` family
#   (TIMETZ, TIME WITH TIME ZONE, TIME WITHOUT TIME
#   ZONE) is NOT in zeta's CAST registry, yet the
#   `TIMESTAMP` family (TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE,
#   TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE) IS. Both TIME and
#   TIMESTAMP are first-class PG types with parallel
#   spellings; zeta has covered only one half of the
#   pair. Migration risk: any PG schema or query that
#   round-trips through TIME WITH TIME ZONE will hit
#   the catch-all `unsupported CAST target type` reject,
#   even though TIMESTAMP-equivalent expressions work.
#   This is the SAME pattern as PR #427's MULTIRANGE
#   gap: parser recognizes the spelling but the runtime
#   CAST dispatcher has no arm. Cross-link
#   [[project_two_layer_type_recognition_pattern]].
#
#   FINDING 2 — PG IDENTIFIER-TYPE PARTIAL-REGISTRY
#   ASYMMETRY. PG's internal identifier types are
#   accepted asymmetrically: `"char"` (PG 1-byte
#   internal type, lowercase, requires double quotes
#   for keyword disambiguation) IS in the CAST
#   registry; `name` (PG 64-byte identifier) and `xid`
#   (PG transaction ID) are NOT. None of these have
#   user-facing utility in zeta — but pg_catalog and
#   information_schema queries from PG client tools
#   routinely cast to these. The same CAST registry
#   that knows `"char"` (a niche type) has skipped
#   `name` (used in every pg_catalog column). NCHAR(n)
#   (SQL Server-ish narrow national char) is also
#   rejected — included here because it shares the
#   identifier-character-type slot of the parser
#   surface and IS accepted by sqlparser at parse
#   time, only to reject at the runtime dispatcher
#   (same plan-vs-runtime asymmetry as BYTEA in
#   cast_target_type_aliases:181). Cross-link
#   [[project_two_layer_type_recognition_pattern]] and
#   [[project_function_arms_gap_closed]] (function-arm
#   shape (c) pattern, now extended to CAST targets).
#
#   FINDING 3 — 6th HAND-ROLLED ERROR FORMATTER
#   WITNESS: `cannot cast to TIMESTAMP`. When the
#   CAST registry HAS the target type but the source
#   value type is incompatible (here: float8
#   `+infinity` → TIMESTAMP), the runtime emits
#   `cannot cast to TIMESTAMP` — a DIFFERENT shape
#   from the `unsupported CAST target type: <type>`
#   family. Critical fingerprints:
#     (a) the target type IS echoed (TIMESTAMP) but
#         the source type is NOT
#     (b) NO `at Line: N, Column: M` suffix
#     (c) verb is `cannot cast`, NOT `unsupported`
#   This places it firmly in the
#   [[project_hand_rolled_error_formatters]] cluster
#   as the 6th witness, joining:
#     1. ON COMMIT smushed-typo (PR #457)
#     2. CREATE FUNCTION body-required (PR #458)
#     3. unsupported DROP STAGE statement (PR #455)
#     4. unsupported DROP ROLE statement (PR #460)
#     5. unsupported DROP USER statement (PR #460)
#     6. cannot cast to TIMESTAMP   (THIS SLICE)
#   Note the previous 5 witnesses are all PARSE-LAYER
#   or DROP-DISPATCHER rejects; THIS one is in the
#   VALUE-CAST runtime — proving the hand-rolled-
#   formatter pattern spans MULTIPLE engine layers,
#   not just the parser/DDL surface. Error-string
#   matchers that key off the Line/Column suffix will
#   NOT match this row.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   The PG CAST surface has FOUR distinct rejection
#   shapes, each pinned in a different SHAPE block:
#     - SHAPE A — TIME-family runtime catch-all
#       `unsupported CAST target type: <T>` (no
#       Line/Column, but ECHOES target spelling
#       verbatim including `WITH TIME ZONE` suffix).
#     - SHAPE B — Identifier-type runtime catch-all
#       (same formatter as A; the asymmetry vs `"char"`
#       SUPPORT pin is what's novel).
#     - SHAPE C — Parse-layer reject for NATIONAL
#       CHARACTER VARYING: errno 1064, `Expected: ),
#       found: CHARACTER at Line: 1, Column: 29`
#       (standard sqlparser whitelist error WITH Line/
#       Column, fundamentally DIFFERENT code path from
#       A/B). NATIONAL is absorbed elsewhere; the
#       `CHARACTER` keyword arrives in an unexpected
#       position.
#     - SHAPE D — Runtime `cannot cast to <T>`
#       formatter (Finding 3 — 6th hand-rolled witness).
#   The same CAST target-type surface takes FOUR error
#   paths; pinning the boundary between them is the
#   point of this slice.
#
# Wire divergence check: probed via mysqlwire (MTR
# canonical, dynamic port reservation). Per
# [[feedback_probe_wire_match_slice_wire]] pgwire was
# NOT separately probed — the CAST registry sits below
# the wire boundary and errors are stable across
# clients.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: TIME-family CAST — `CAST(x AS TIMETZ)`,
#     `CAST(x AS TIME WITH TIME ZONE)`, `CAST(x AS TIME
#     WITHOUT TIME ZONE)` all error 1105 `unsupported
#     CAST target type: <T>`. Use plain `CAST(x AS
#     TIMESTAMP)` instead — the TIMESTAMP family DOES
#     work (see SHAPE A SUPPORT pin). If time-zone
#     handling matters, route through a TIMESTAMP and
#     extract the time portion via DATE_PART / EXTRACT.
#   # zeta-adapt: PG identifier-type CAST — `CAST(x AS
#     name)` and `CAST(x AS xid)` error 1105
#     `unsupported CAST target type: <T>`. Use TEXT
#     instead. pg_catalog / information_schema queries
#     that cast through these types will need
#     rewriting.
#   # zeta-adapt: NCHAR(n) — `CAST(x AS NCHAR(n))`
#     errors 1105 `unsupported CAST target type:
#     NCHAR(n)`. Use CHAR(n) or VARCHAR(n).
#   # zeta-adapt: NATIONAL CHARACTER VARYING(n) — the
#     parser rejects this at parse time (1064,
#     `Expected: ), found: CHARACTER`). Use VARCHAR(n)
#     or CHARACTER VARYING(n) — both work per cast_
#     target_type_aliases:117. The NATIONAL keyword
#     itself is partially absorbed; the trailing
#     CHARACTER lands in the wrong grammar slot.
#   # zeta-adapt: float8 → TIMESTAMP — `CAST(x::float8
#     AS TIMESTAMP)` errors 1105 `cannot cast to
#     TIMESTAMP` (DIFFERENT shape from the unsupported-
#     target rejects above; this is a value-cast
#     surface message). PG accepts `'infinity'::float8`
#     as a TIMESTAMP via the special infinity sentinel;
#     zeta does not. Route via `to_timestamp(x)` if
#     epoch-style conversion is what's wanted.

--echo === SHAPE A — TIME-family CAST-registry gap (3 rejects + 1 TIMESTAMP-family SUPPORT) ===

# TIMETZ is the PG short-spelling for TIME WITH TIME
# ZONE. zeta's parser accepts it at parse time (no
# 1064 parse-reject) but the runtime CAST dispatcher
# has no arm — catch-all formatter ECHOES the bare
# spelling.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('12:00' AS TIMETZ);

# Long-form TIME WITH TIME ZONE — same gap, ECHOES
# the full multi-keyword spelling including WITH TIME
# ZONE suffix.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('12:00:00+05:00' AS TIME WITH TIME ZONE);

# TIME WITHOUT TIME ZONE — completes the TIME family.
# The presence of TWO TIME variants (with/without
# time zone) plus the short alias TIMETZ all missing
# from the registry shows the gap is FAMILY-WIDE, not
# a single-keyword omission.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('12:00:00' AS TIME WITHOUT TIME ZONE);

# SUPPORT pin: TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE — the
# parallel TIMESTAMP family spelling DOES work. This
# is the asymmetry frame for Finding 1: same SQL-
# standard syntactic family, opposite CAST-registry
# coverage. If a future change unifies TIME +
# TIMESTAMP into one dispatcher arm this SUPPORT pin
# will keep working AND the three rejects above must
# be flipped to SUPPORT.
SELECT CAST('2024-01-01 12:00:00' AS TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE) AS timestamp_without_tz_works;

--echo === SHAPE B — PG identifier-type CAST-registry gap (3 rejects + 1 "char" SUPPORT) ===

# NCHAR(n) — SQL Server narrow-national-char-ish.
# Accepted at parse time, rejected at runtime — the
# same plan-vs-runtime asymmetry as BYTEA in
# cast_target_type_aliases:181. The parens form
# carries through verbatim in the error message.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('a' AS NCHAR(5));

# `name` — PG 64-byte internal identifier type. Every
# pg_catalog column that holds a name (relname,
# attname, proname, etc.) is of this type. NOT in the
# CAST registry; client tools that cast through `name`
# will hit this reject.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('a' AS name);

# `xid` — PG transaction ID type. Surfaces in
# pg_locks, pg_stat_activity, and txid_current()
# queries. NOT in the CAST registry.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('1/1' AS xid);

# SUPPORT pin: `"char"` (lowercase, double-quoted to
# disambiguate from the CHAR keyword) is PG's 1-byte
# internal type — niche, used in pg_catalog flag
# columns like pg_class.relkind. zeta HAS this arm in
# the CAST registry. NULL → NULL is the safest probe
# (no source-value coverage to inadvertently
# duplicate). If a future change adds `name` and
# `xid` to the registry this SUPPORT pin will keep
# working AND the three rejects above must be flipped
# to SUPPORT.
SELECT CAST(NULL AS "char") AS char_internal_works;

--echo === SHAPE C — NATIONAL CHARACTER VARYING parse-layer reject (1 reject) ===

# DIFFERENT code path from SHAPE A/B: this rejects at
# PARSE time (errno 1064, with Line/Column suffix)
# instead of at the runtime CAST dispatcher. The
# NATIONAL keyword is partially absorbed by the
# parser's CAST target grammar; the trailing
# `CHARACTER` keyword arrives in an unexpected
# position (where `)` was expected). Pins the
# fingerprint:
#   `Expected: ), found: CHARACTER at Line: 1,
#    Column: 29`
# Plain `CHARACTER VARYING(10)` (without NATIONAL)
# works per cast_target_type_aliases:117; only the
# NATIONAL prefix triggers this parse-reject.
--error 1064
SELECT CAST('a' AS NATIONAL CHARACTER VARYING(10));

--echo === SHAPE D — hand-rolled `cannot cast to TIMESTAMP` formatter (1 reject, 6th witness) ===

# Distinct from the catch-all `unsupported CAST
# target type: <T>` formatter used in SHAPES A/B:
# here the target TIMESTAMP IS in the registry, but
# the runtime CAST dispatcher rejects the source
# value type (float8) with a DIFFERENT bespoke
# message: `cannot cast to TIMESTAMP`. Fingerprints:
#   (a) target type ECHOED (TIMESTAMP)
#   (b) source type NOT echoed (no float8)
#   (c) NO `at Line: N, Column: M` suffix
#   (d) verb `cannot cast` (vs `unsupported`)
# These four fingerprints together place this in the
# [[project_hand_rolled_error_formatters]] cluster
# as the 6th witness. Note: the previous 5 witnesses
# were all parser-layer or DDL-dispatcher rejects;
# this one is in the runtime VALUE-CAST surface,
# proving the hand-rolled-formatter pattern spans
# multiple engine layers (not just the parser/DDL
# corner). PG accepts `'infinity'::float8` as a
# TIMESTAMP via its infinity sentinel; zeta does not.
--error 1105
SELECT CAST('+infinity'::float8 AS TIMESTAMP);
